  "problem_statement": "Dict class breaks when not inheriting from dict\n\n#### Description\n\nThe Dict class no longer works properly when it doesn't inherit from the built-in `dict` class. Basic operations like creating instances, accessing items, and using dict methods fail with various errors including RecursionError and AttributeError.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom addict import Dict\n\n# Basic instantiation fails\nd = Dict()\nprint(d)  # Should work but raises AttributeError\n\n# Setting and getting items fails\nd = Dict()\nd.test = 'value'  # Raises AttributeError\nprint(d.test)\n\n# Dict methods don't work\nd = Dict({'a': 1})  # Raises AttributeError\nd.update({'b': 2})  # Raises TypeError\n\n# isinstance check fails\nd = Dict()\nprint(isinstance(d, dict))  # Returns False instead of True\n```\n\nThe errors include:\n- `AttributeError: 'super' object has no attribute 'keys'`\n- `RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded`\n- `TypeError: 'Dict' object is not subscriptable`\n\nThis breaks all basic functionality of the Dict class."
}